A negative zero float. TOML accepts it; this format does not, because
`-0.0` is a second spelling of `0.0` and a regime is identified by the
settings it names. The float is built through the record's decimal
constructor, which refuses the second spelling on both sides of the point.

`-0` as an INTEGER is refused too, by `negative-zero.toml` beside this file.
An earlier version of this reason said it was accepted and called the
asymmetry "real and stated in the grammar". There is no asymmetry: the
integer terminal has refused `-0` since it moved to `number.pest`, and the
grammar comment that was cited as stating the asymmetry said the same false
thing until it was corrected on 2026-09-12. A `.reason` is only read when its
fixture unexpectedly parses, so nothing in the gate compares one to the tree
-- which is how this sentence outlived the rule it described.
